{% extends "base.html.jinja" %}
{% block content %}
<h1>Privacy policy</h1>
{% if ga4_measurement_id %}
<p class="lede">
  The {{ site_name }} website uses Google Analytics 4 to count visits and see
  which pages are read, unless your browser sends Global Privacy Control or
  Do Not Track, or you opt out. The {{ site_name }} iOS app collects no data at all: no
  analytics, no tracking, and no third-party SDK. Neither has an account or
  advertising.
</p>
{% else %}
<p class="lede">
  Neither the {{ site_name }} website nor its iOS app collects personal data. There is no
  account, no analytics, no advertising, no tracking, and no third-party
  script or SDK in either.
</p>
{% endif %}
<p class="muted">Last updated 2026-09-17. Covers this website and the {{ site_name }} iOS app.</p>

<h2 id="site">The website</h2>
{% if ga4_measurement_id %}
<ul>
  <li><strong>Google Analytics 4.</strong> Each page loads Google's analytics
      script (gtag.js, from googletagmanager.com). It records the page you
      view, the page you came from, your browser, device type, screen size
      and language, your approximate location (which Google works out from
      your IP address; Google Analytics 4 does not store the IP address
      itself), and interactions such as scrolling and clicks on links to
      other sites. It tells repeat visits apart with a random identifier kept
      in first-party <code>_ga</code> cookies.</li>
  <li><strong>Global Privacy Control and Do Not Track are honored.</strong>
      If your browser sends either signal, the analytics script is never
      loaded: nothing is sent to Google and no analytics cookie is set.</li>
  <li id="opt-out"><strong>Opting out on this device.</strong> The
      &ldquo;Opt out of analytics&rdquo; button at the bottom of every page
      turns Google Analytics off in this browser. Every page you open
      afterwards checks for that choice before loading Google Analytics, and
      so does not load it at all. The choice is saved in this
      browser&rsquo;s local storage for this site, not in a cookie, and is
      never sent anywhere. The button then reads &ldquo;Opt back in&rdquo;;
      press it to undo the choice. Clearing this site&rsquo;s data in your
      browser clears the choice too. Google Analytics cookies set before you
      opted out stay in your browser until they expire or you clear them,
      but nothing sends them to Google while you are opted out.</li>
  <li><strong>The EEA, the UK and Switzerland:</strong> analytics cookies are
      denied by default there, using Google's Consent Mode. Google Analytics
      then sets no cookie and receives only cookieless measurements.</li>
  <li><strong>No advertising use.</strong> Google signals and ad
      personalization are turned off, and the advertising consent signals
      (ad storage, ad user data, ad personalization) are denied everywhere.
      There are no ads on this site.</li>
  <li><strong>Retention:</strong> Google Analytics keeps event-level data for
      14 months, then deletes it. Aggregated reports, such as page-view
      totals, are kept longer.</li>
  <li><strong>Google's terms:</strong>
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites">how
      Google uses information from sites that use its services</a> and
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy
      policy</a>. Google also offers an
      <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout">opt-out browser
      add-on</a> for Google Analytics.</li>
  <li>Calendar feeds (.ics) are plain files with no analytics; your calendar
      app downloads them directly. This site does not know who has
      subscribed.</li>
  <li>No account, no email address, no sign-up, anywhere on this site.</li>
  <li>The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. Like any web host, GitHub receives
      standard request information (IP address, the page requested, time,
      browser type) to serve pages &mdash; see GitHub's
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">privacy
      statement</a>.</li>
</ul>
{% else %}
<ul>
  <li>Every page is a plain static file. No cookies are set and no JavaScript
      runs.</li>
  <li>No page makes a request to any server other than the one serving it.</li>
  <li>Calendar feeds (.ics) are plain files; your calendar app downloads them
      directly. This site does not know who has subscribed.</li>
  <li>The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. Like any web host, GitHub receives
      standard request information (IP address, the page requested, time,
      browser type) to serve pages &mdash; see GitHub's
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">privacy
      statement</a>. This site adds no collection on top of that.</li>
</ul>
{% endif %}

<h2 id="app">The iOS app</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No analytics:</strong> the app has no analytics, no tracking and
      no third-party SDK.{% if ga4_measurement_id %} The website's Google
      Analytics does not run inside the app.{% endif %}</li>
  <li><strong>What stays on your phone:</strong> the waters you favorite, the
      latest copy of the planting schedule, and a record of which listings the
      app has already alerted you about. These live only in the app's own
      storage on your device (and in your device backups, if you back up your
      phone). Deleting the app deletes them. They are never sent to the
      developer or anyone else.</li>
  <li><strong>The one network request:</strong> the app downloads the public
      planting-schedule file this website publishes, from GitHub Pages. The
      request carries no identifier and nothing about you or your favorites;
      as with any web request, the host sees the IP address it came from.
      Links to CDFW's map or to this site open in your browser{% if ga4_measurement_id %},
      where this site's Google Analytics applies as described above{% endif %}.</li>
  <li><strong>Notifications:</strong> alerts are local notifications that iOS
      schedules on your device. There is no push service and no device
      token.</li>
  <li><strong>Purchases:</strong> the one-time in-app purchase is handled
      entirely by Apple. The developer does not receive your name, email
      address or payment details; Apple's privacy policy applies to the
      transaction.</li>
  <li><strong>App Store privacy label:</strong> Data Not Collected.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="children">Children</h2>
{% if ga4_measurement_id %}
<p>
  The app collects nothing from anyone, including children. The website's
  analytics are the same for every visitor and never ask for a name, age or
  contact details.
</p>
{% else %}
<p>Nothing is collected from anyone, including children.</p>
{% endif %}

<h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
<p>If this policy changes, this page changes, and the date above with it.</p>

<h2 id="contact">Questions</h2>
{% if support_email %}
<p>Email <a href="mailto:{{ support_email }}">{{ support_email }}</a>.</p>
{% else %}
<p>See <a href="{{ root }}support/">support &amp; FAQ</a>.</p>
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
